WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:07.920
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.080
I am Kristina Talbert-Slagle,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.100
assistant professor of
general internal medicine,

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.310
and associate director
at the Yale Institute

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.640
for Global Health and the
Yale School of Medicine.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.800
I am delighted to welcome you

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.940
to the Yale Institute for Global
Health conversation series

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.200
a venue for our community
to learn from and connect

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.780
with innovative leaders in global health.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.450
Coming from diverse backgrounds,

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.410
our invited guests will introduce us

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.920
to cutting edge work and global health,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.460
including research,
implementation, policy,

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.830
and many other perspectives.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.900
In addition to the Yale
Institute of global health

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.520
we are indebted to the George
Herbert Walker Jr. lecture

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.970
and international studies

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:54.460
at the Whitney and Betty MacMillan Center

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.070
for International and Area Studies at Yale

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.380
for co-sponsoring this series,
including today's event.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.740
Today, I am pleased to be joined

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.530
by my co-moderator Dr. Onyema Ogbuagu,

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.330
associate professor of
medicine and director

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:13.820
of HIV clinical trials at
the Yale School of Medicine.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.563
Welcome Onyema, it is great
to have you with us today.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:18.520
- Thank you, Kristina.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.870
- And now, let me introduce
today's distinguished guest,

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:27.840
Dr. Bernice Dahn, the former
minister of health in Liberia

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.770
and current vice president
for health sciences

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.000
at the University of Liberia.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.670
Dr. Dahn is renowned for her work,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.780
rebuilding Liberia
postwar healthcare system.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.780
The second Liberian civil
war from 1999 to 2003

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.460
decimated Liberia's health infrastructure

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.740
and compelled many of its
health workers to flee.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.090
As deputy minister for health services

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:56.500
and chief medical officer,
Dr. Dahn was integral

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.500
in bringing into Liberia's
2015 Ebola outbreak.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.080
She developed treatment and
infection prevention protocols

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.300
organized training, mobilized resources

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.490
and restored routine
health service delivery.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:16.700
Critical steps that likely
saved thousands of lives,

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.310
after Ebola as minister
of health, Dr. Dahn

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.680
created a seven year initiative

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.270
to improve and expand the
public health workforce.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.280
And she developed with partners,

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.240
Liberia's first national
cancer control strategy,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.240
in her current role as vice
president for health sciences

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.370
at the University of Liberia,

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.490
Dr. Dahn spearheaded, the
critical revitalization

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.470
of medical nursing and midwifery schools,

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.610
creating a new educational
and training pathway

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.030
for healthcare professionals
in the country.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.143
Welcome Dr. Dahn, it is an
honor to have you with us today.

00:02:56.229 --> 00:02:58.007
- Thank you, Kristina.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.763
And good afternoon to everybody.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:07.770
It is good to connect once
more with the US community,

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:12.810
and everybody who is on
this in this session.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.850
I hope that we are being able to cope well

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:22.980
with COVID and we can
move our lives forward.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:24.920
We'll also like to congratulate Onyema

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.180
who is working seriously to support some

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.920
of the COVID prevention activities

00:03:31.995 --> 00:03:35.280
the vaccine, production, trials

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.440
and we are looking forward
to results from there

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.840
so it can put us at ease again.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.210
Welcome to everybody and I am here

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.663
to listen and answer your
questions, thank you.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.990
- Thank you so much, Dr. Dahn.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:56.850
So Onyema and I, have
prepared a few questions.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.410
We also welcome questions
from the audience,

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:00.880
please put them through the chat

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.280
and we will look forward to
sharing them with Dr. Dahn,

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.940
as we go along in this conversation.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.870
So Dr. Dahn, let me
just start by asking you

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:14.440
can you speak to how you
approach to the concept

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:18.580
of systems rebuilding in
a post-crisis setting?

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:22.310
Was that response different
for different types of crises

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.440
such as a pandemic versus a civil war,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.803
and how did you achieve political
buy-in for the response?

00:04:33.224 --> 00:04:34.574
- Okay, thank you Kristina.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.060
Liberia's healthcare system

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:45.687
have been repeatedly challenged by crisis.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.963
So from the civil war, the Ebola,

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.653
post Ebola and now COVID.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.980
Rebuilding for Liberia
has been in to folds,

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:05.880
from the crisis and then the setbacks

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.553
that was caused by the Ebola outbreak.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:16.350
In any of those situations,
strong government leadership

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.223
is very important.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.830
There's a need for a well defined

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.913
and well articulated vision.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:33.290
The vision can not be in your
head for the entire country,

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:37.870
so it has to be documented
and it has to be accessible

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.290
to everybody, including the locals

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.940
those who it will benefit all
the different stakeholders

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:46.293
including those who support
you to implement them.

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:51.070
Now, in the Liberia context,

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.890
and I've seen this globally also,

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:59.890
when there's a change from
one government administration

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.240
to another, it just a perfect
opportunity to articulate

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:11.910
visions and position,
especially if you are working

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.557
in this sector up to
position the sectors vision,

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:19.070
because at that time, the government

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:23.020
and all its partners, and well wishers

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.690
who will support the government to grow,

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.270
looking up to the
government for leadership

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:36.040
for them to make decisions.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:37.233
For example,

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.350
normally the government will be defining

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:48.350
their 90 days deliverables,
150 days deliverables.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:54.260
And that is a perfect
opportunity to articulate

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:59.260
to use that timing to position the sector.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:04.420
So the Liberia health sector at the time

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:09.420
took advantage of that and
at the time coming from war,

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:12.310
the human resources were not there,

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:16.150
but we also had partners
who were on the ground

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:21.150
who were also supporting the government

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:24.410
during the emergency and was
looking up to the government

00:07:26.017 --> 00:07:31.017
during the rebuilding process
on roles that they could play.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:34.420
So we leveraged that partnership

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.790
using both resources, human and financial

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:45.240
to develop our first
national policy and strategy.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.723
So the policy and
strategy was co-designed,

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:52.480
with the governments in the leadership,

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.550
the government, it is
important to emphasize

00:07:56.427 --> 00:07:58.504
that the government has to provide

00:07:58.504 --> 00:08:00.363
true leadership for ownership.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.240
And so with that, for us

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:06.770
it helped to build confidence

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:11.160
between us and the partners,
but more importantly,

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:16.153
because the policy and those
instruments were co-designed,

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:21.120
the partners also positioned themselves

00:08:22.106 --> 00:08:26.443
as to how they can help the
government to deliver things

00:08:26.443 --> 00:08:29.720
that we all agreed on in there.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:33.193
Remember, Liberia is a
resource limited country,

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.810
the entire government budget is less

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:41.433
than 600 million up to today's date.

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:46.720
The ministry of health or
the health sector budget

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:53.530
for most time range, between
12 to 14% of the total budget,

00:08:55.180 --> 00:09:00.180
about 70 to 80% of that budget
pays health workers salary.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:06.360
And so that was like, not much left

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.830
to even start building the systems

00:09:10.656 --> 00:09:12.300
that you are there to build.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:17.300
So there was a need to work
and leverage partnerships

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:21.930
both local and international to be able

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.600
to deliver the strategy and the plan.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.520
So that means we develop a framework

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:29.733
within which who are going to work.

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:36.230
The Ministry of Health also developed

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:41.090
some financial planning
tools for the health sector.

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:43.760
So, in developing those tools

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:45.520
remember that we're coming from war,

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.110
most of our professionals
have left the country,

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:51.670
there were very few persons in
country who knew what to do.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:54.910
So again, we co-designed those tools,

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:58.540
the financial planning tools.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:01.710
So their plan itself was costed,

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:05.653
costing the plan helped to
build additional confidence.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:11.610
Partners knew, and even
the government knew

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:16.010
at least how much it was going
to take to deliver the plan.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:21.010
And so that helped for people
to mobilize the resources

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.743
and plan to support its implementation.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:32.740
The tools also helped
to map donor support.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.480
So we were able to map who
the donors were in country,

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:44.913
where they were supporting
and what they were supporting.

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.730
And from the initial stages you could see

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:53.220
clearly there were overlaps, duplications

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:57.260
and at that time they were supporting the

00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:59.563
implementing partners directly.

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.823
So with the resource mapping tool,

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.223
we engaged the donors.

00:11:07.223 --> 00:11:09.353
We were at first and I remember

00:11:09.353 --> 00:11:11.245
we designed the plans together.

00:11:11.245 --> 00:11:13.720
So we were friends and we had

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:15.340
to come back together to ensure

00:11:16.227 --> 00:11:20.530
that what we all put in the
plan, we were supporting that.

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.720
So we used the resource mapping tool

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:27.810
to do geographic redistribution
of the resources.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:29.907
So we negotiated with the donors,

00:11:31.644 --> 00:11:35.650
to support where we
think we had serious gaps

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:36.963
and we need their help.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:39.923
And so that was done,

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:44.653
and that helped to bridge
some of the equity gap also.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:49.433
Now that system was moving on well,

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:52.703
I believe we all know that,

00:11:54.624 --> 00:11:56.370
Liberia was able to achieve

00:11:56.370 --> 00:12:01.220
the under five mortality
reduction, the MDGs

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:05.470
within three years before the end date.

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:07.520
Even though we started at six years late,

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:10.343
then we had the Ebola outbreak.

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:15.883
The Ebola outbreak, the scale
of the outbreak took over.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:20.180
Before the outbreak, in the initial plan

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:25.180
we had a unit designed,
so respond to emergencies

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:31.290
and so whenever there
was like Measles outbreak

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:35.803
Yellow Fever, we sent a team
to the county to support them.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:41.050
With the outbreak of Ebola because we had,

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:42.680
we were all working together.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:46.870
We had a coordination
forum that met quarterly,

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:48.860
and another technical working forum

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:51.083
that met monthly.

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:55.060
We called the donors back

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:59.480
and together we redesigned
the emergency system.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:04.480
So the IMS the incident
management system was established,

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:08.280
emergency operations
centers were established

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:10.319
in the counties and they were supported

00:13:10.319 --> 00:13:11.993
with the things that they needed.

00:13:11.993 --> 00:13:16.993
The surveillance system was
established, also to respond

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:22.340
these systems that were
developed in response,

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:26.730
so the crisis transition to
more permanent institutions

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:30.403
or procedures with increasing stability.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:36.300
And so the systems we established
during the Ebola outbreak

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:42.860
we transitioned it to the
national public health institutes

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:48.630
with all its appendices, still functioning

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:53.290
and it functioned, so it
had the main responsibility

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:58.290
off identifying diseases
that had epidemic potential

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:00.653
and responding to them.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:06.623
And so you are realize
that during the outbreak,

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:11.800
we had to reactivate the
routine healthcare services.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.630
And then we had the emergency system,

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:17.040
the two co-existed at some point

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.513
and that's the system that is working now.

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:23.700
So with COVID the outbreak of COVID,

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:28.600
it was very easy to
reactivate the emergency,

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:33.200
the IMS, reacting with
the EOC in the 15 counties

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:39.690
the surveillance team was
also already functioning

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:42.523
so it was a matter of
increasing surge capacity.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.850
With the COVID, the team already knew

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:51.110
it is a disease of epidemic potential.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:53.453
And looking at our situation,

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:57.340
it wasn't necessarily to isolate

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:00.880
and treat isolate people
who were infected.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:03.530
All, everybody who was
infected was isolated

00:15:03.530 --> 00:15:05.770
and treated in the treatment unit,

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:08.880
everybody who was a contact was isolated

00:15:10.725 --> 00:15:14.101
and monitored for the 14 days or so

00:15:14.101 --> 00:15:15.360
before they were discharged.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:20.360
So building a system, the
approach was not too different,

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.960
the strategies, policy,
even in the emergency

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:31.660
we developed plans and strategies.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:37.630
We aligned the partners and
all those things we done,

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.210
the implementation to those.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:43.250
So we use the partners based
on your comparative advantage,

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:46.297
so if you are a donor,
you support the donor

00:15:46.297 --> 00:15:49.623
co-ordination and things
that we needed to implement.

00:15:51.873 --> 00:15:53.590
If you are an implementing partner,

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:55.160
you supported a county health team

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:58.570
where you were with the
emergency operations center

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:01.893
and all the way down
to the community level.

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:07.960
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:09.630
again, always nice to talk with you.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:11.900
So, I want to follow up on that question.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:15.400
So, we do know that while
rebuilding health systems

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:19.070
it's really important to
consider human capital.

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:21.230
And we know Liberia has significant

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:22.720
healthcare workforce shortages.

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:25.625
You know, you've had
the inglorious position

00:16:25.625 --> 00:16:26.590
of having some of the lowest ratios,

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:30.990
of healthcare workers to
population in the world due to,

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:32.610
and also exacerbated by some

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:34.070
of the crises you mentioned earlier

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:36.410
the civil war and the Ebola outbreak.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:39.690
So, my question to you
is how do you address

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:42.870
rebuilding the healthcare workforce and,

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:45.650
having known of some of your
work we'd love to hear also

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:47.540
about the medical and health sciences

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.000
educational programs that you've initiated

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:52.913
to address the pipeline
for healthcare workers.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:56.277
- Okay, thank you.

00:16:58.942 --> 00:17:03.807
The vision to develop fit
for purpose health workforce

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:10.720
was articulated well
in our investment plan

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:13.110
for building a resilient
healthcare delivery system

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:15.580
after the Ebola outbreak.

00:17:15.580 --> 00:17:20.580
So that was a part of the
overall national vision

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:22.883
from the beginning.

00:17:27.939 --> 00:17:31.570
So during the outbreak, we, first of all

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:34.319
remember that we were coming from crisis.

00:17:34.319 --> 00:17:37.040
We had, they had the health workforce

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:42.040
and then health workers died
during the outbreak also.

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:46.420
So, there was a need to actually look

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:48.500
at how we build a workforce

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:53.541
and normally we do these things

00:17:53.541 --> 00:17:55.868
everything is on the
line by an assessment.

00:17:55.868 --> 00:17:56.850
So it's informed by data,

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:59.193
so we first did an assessment.

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:04.508
The assessment informed
us on the health workers

00:18:04.508 --> 00:18:06.703
that we had in acute shortages.

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:11.380
And those that health workforce

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:13.800
that we needed to improve quality.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:18.800
So if you take the physicians
and the midwives, for example

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:25.631
they findings was for
us to improve quality,

00:18:25.631 --> 00:18:30.631
expand the skill mix,
and increase the numbers,

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:38.780
as compared to the nursing
where the finding was

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:42.400
and the recommendation was for us

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:44.960
to improve the quality of training

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:50.793
and increase their skill mix.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:56.003
So, with this strategy,
with the assessment,

00:18:57.860 --> 00:19:01.890
we decided to develop the strategy.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:03.833
And this time around,

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:10.010
we came across a strategy
that was similar strategy

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:15.950
for Rwanda that partner in
country Rwanda to develop.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:21.790
So we engaged that partner
and that is (mumbles)

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:26.790
to see how they could help the
Ministry of Health to design.

00:19:29.498 --> 00:19:30.670
They help to bring all the other partners

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:33.810
on the table to design the
health workforce strategy.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:37.370
So that went through a similar process

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:40.400
like the national policy and plan.

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:45.400
So we engaged all the
stakeholders around the table,

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:48.454
we presented the assessment reports

00:19:48.454 --> 00:19:51.325
and we used the report to develop

00:19:51.325 --> 00:19:54.393
what we call the health
workforce strategy.

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:59.160
Now, with that strategy, the
strategy was also costed,

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:04.053
and so we engaged the partners
to support the strategy,

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:07.090
they were on the table to develop it.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:10.800
So a good, they had already
positioned themselves

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.389
on how they were going to support it.

00:20:13.389 --> 00:20:16.740
So USAID for instance decided

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:19.930
to support the mid-level trainings

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:23.093
that we had in the strategy documents.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:26.013
So the support that supporter

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:31.580
and some other UN US-based NGOs

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:34.230
to work with those training institutions,

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:38.370
midwifery, nursing to really
look at the curriculum

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:41.750
to look at the training resources,

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:45.010
the faculty, and the
systems that will help

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:50.010
to build the educations systems
for the health workforce.

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:53.993
So they have did that
for the mid-level cutter,

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:59.030
then we engaged the World Bank.

00:20:59.030 --> 00:21:04.030
At the time, we were seeing
the decline of the Ebola cases

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:09.990
and we had the Ebola
resources from the bank.

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:13.689
And so we engaged the
World Bank to reprogram

00:21:13.689 --> 00:21:18.689
to support the health workforce strategy.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:21.560
And because they had a strategy,
which was the instrument

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:23.753
that defined the government priorities,

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:26.650
they agreed they were going

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:28.893
to support the medical education.

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:34.280
Now the medical education in
that strategy was in two-folds

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:37.383
the undergraduate program,

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:41.810
which did not shut down during the war,

00:21:41.810 --> 00:21:45.163
but the quality was
compromised significantly.

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:48.610
And at that time it was graduating

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:51.703
like 200 persons per year,

00:21:51.703 --> 00:21:54.753
and we needed to scale up the numbers.

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:02.290
Then we did not have the faculty to teach,

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:05.213
the few physicians who were in country

00:22:05.213 --> 00:22:07.630
were the ones doing the teaching

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:09.253
and doing the clinical work.

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:13.110
And so that also helped to compromise

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:15.483
the quality of the training even further.

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:20.820
And so we decided, and then we had a,

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:24.440
we didn't have their
residency training at all.

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:28.610
So at that time, the assessment report

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:33.000
I don't think we even had
10 specialists in country

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:36.137
for any of the specialties.

00:22:36.137 --> 00:22:39.760
Even if you put all these
specialties together

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.393
there's some total was not 10 persons.

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:46.470
And so we, the World Bank decided

00:22:47.554 --> 00:22:49.843
to support the medical education.

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:55.890
We initially set up the
Liberia College of Physicians,

00:22:55.890 --> 00:22:57.872
we set up the Liberia College
of Physicians and Surgeons

00:22:57.872 --> 00:23:00.160
because we decided the training

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:02.140
will follow the West African model

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.423
and any West African model,

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:10.930
the basic medical training
is with the university.

00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:14.360
So that remains with the
University of Liberia,

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:16.373
and the residency training is,

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:21.240
it could be directly under
the West Africa college

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:23.840
or a local college, so
we established it on

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:28.840
under the local college and
I still West African college

00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:33.980
to monitor so it can be linked

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:36.523
to the West African College model.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:39.740
So we did that.

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:42.770
Then we built partnership with

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:46.660
first, we decided to recruit faculty.

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:48.590
And so the LCPS as the Liberian College

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:50.110
of Physicians and Surgeons recruited

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:53.990
the first set of faculty
for the first year,

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:56.420
there were challenges because once you go

00:23:56.420 --> 00:24:00.200
on an individual basis
after the first year

00:24:00.200 --> 00:24:02.100
then they put their cast on the table,

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:07.980
salaries they felt were low
and they started negotiating

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:10.960
and that was also a problem for us.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:15.960
So then we decided to switch
to engage institutions instead,

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:21.160
so build partnership with
training institutions

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:26.160
universities, they
helped to recruit for us

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:29.400
from within their network that way

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:30.960
we don't have to deal with individual,

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:34.640
we deal with institutions and then

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:37.970
they can deal with the individuals.

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:42.970
So we did that, then
most of the institutions

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:47.170
that we build a partnership with

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:51.720
were the you where US universities.

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:56.720
We tried the universities
within the region

00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:00.390
but they were not as organized as it is

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:02.410
with the US universities.

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:05.410
And some of these US
universities were also

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:08.010
supporting other universities
within the region.

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:09.893
So they already had the platform.

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:15.700
So we negotiated with the US
universities to recruit people

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:19.083
from the region since the
curriculum is from the region.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:24.210
So they agree to that,
and so the recruitment

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:27.220
of the faculty, most of
them came from the region

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:31.100
except where we needed maybe
subspecialties specialties.

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:35.736
Sometimes they brought in faculty members

00:25:35.736 --> 00:25:38.460
from US universities.

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:43.153
The beauty of that relationship,

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:45.930
faculty members were recruited

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:48.470
to come and teach in an institution

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:52.833
where, we didn't even have
the equipment to teach.

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.220
So the US universities position themselves

00:25:57.220 --> 00:26:01.110
to mobilize their resources,
to help to teach also.

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:03.773
And a sample is,

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:12.130
we had one of the universities
was responsible for

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:17.283
to recruit faculty, to teach
surgery and to do pathology.

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:20.203
We did not have a pathology lab,

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:22.910
we brought in a pathologist

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:25.023
and there was no laboratory to teach.

00:26:25.990 --> 00:26:28.220
And so (mumbles) who was responsible

00:26:29.167 --> 00:26:32.993
for that actually worked
on mobilizing equipment

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:38.510
and supplies so set up the pathology lab.

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:42.203
And today we have the
modern pathology lab.

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:49.030
Now, we also do not have the capacity to

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:52.200
for curriculum development.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:54.650
There are very few, one
or two persons in country

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:58.590
for the undergraduate program,

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:00.750
we decided we were reforming,

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:05.670
first, we recruited preclinical
faculty from the region

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:07.670
since the clinical faculty share

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:10.603
with the residency
training program faculty.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:16.640
So the faculty from the
region are still with us

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:20.930
and we decided we'll reform
the medical school curriculum

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:26.080
reforming the curriculum, we
are not curriculum experts

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:31.030
but we engage the institutions,

00:27:31.030 --> 00:27:34.490
the partner institutions to assist us

00:27:34.490 --> 00:27:36.193
with the curriculum reform.

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:40.050
And they consented because
they work in institution

00:27:40.050 --> 00:27:44.400
that have units that
look at their curriculum.

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:48.943
And so they work with us
and the curriculum experts,

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:51.853
so we formed the curriculum.

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:56.193
So, we divided our teams in departments,

00:27:57.280 --> 00:28:00.173
we do all the work and weekly,

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:05.480
we have a Zoom meeting to
discuss the work we have done

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:07.440
and they give in their inputs

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:09.483
and that curriculum is coming on well.

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:16.260
Now, currently the medical
education is four years

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:23.000
undergraduate, and then at
five years medical training.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:28.800
So you do a B.sc for us in a
biology, chemistry, or zoology.

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:30.670
And then you enroll
into the medical school

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:33.630
for another five years,
which is nine years.

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:36.900
Liberia happened to be the only country

00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:40.690
in the region that has a
nine-year training program,

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:42.900
others are six years.

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:45.480
So we decided to reform the curriculum

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:47.003
to a seven-year curriculum.

00:28:48.290 --> 00:28:50.373
It should be an integrated curriculum,

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:56.520
competency-based curriculum,
and that is been done well.

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:03.160
Now, in the curriculum
development process,

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:08.060
we know that the university
processes have to approve.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:11.520
And one big concern in country is that

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:14.550
the sciences in from high school is weak.

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:16.820
We decided to do a seven-year curriculum,

00:29:16.820 --> 00:29:21.530
so it's a one year pre-med in
the six years medical school

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:29.090
A one year pre-med,
three years preclinical,

00:29:29.090 --> 00:29:30.283
three years clinical.

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:36.370
We know that if that curriculum goals

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:39.973
to the academic coordination
committee for approval.

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:45.060
Their first question or
their first concern will be

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:48.250
students coming from high school

00:29:48.250 --> 00:29:51.140
have a deficiency in sciences.

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:54.173
During the war years, there
were no science teachers.

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:59.453
So that has been a very
big gap in the training,

00:30:00.310 --> 00:30:04.460
it is known that's the
ministry of education

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:06.793
is building the sciences gradually.

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:11.350
So we decided to build in that process

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:14.003
a science camp for high school students.

00:30:14.870 --> 00:30:19.620
So we'll do that two years before the ends

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:21.610
of the pre-med to make
them most successful

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:23.513
in passing the exams and then become

00:30:23.513 --> 00:30:28.160
more successful in pre-med
and the medical school.

00:30:28.160 --> 00:30:31.653
And that will help to
reduce attrition rates,

00:30:31.653 --> 00:30:34.490
cause at the moment, the
attrition rate is high.

00:30:34.490 --> 00:30:36.660
Sometimes, we start with 60 students

00:30:36.660 --> 00:30:41.417
and by the end of the five
years, you have like 35.

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:48.175
- Okay, thank you, Dr. Dahn,

00:30:48.175 --> 00:30:50.890
I just wanted to make sure
that I didn't step over you.

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:54.150
That's fascinating and it's
wonderful to hear you talk

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:56.010
about all this work that
we have the opportunity

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:59.148
to do in some ways in
partnership with you.

00:30:59.148 --> 00:31:02.870
I wanted to pivot a little bit

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:07.770
because we have noticed,
of course in Liberia

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:11.980
there's an observable contradiction
in the status of women.

00:31:11.980 --> 00:31:14.620
We see some women like yourself

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:19.480
in high positions of
authority, very strong leaders

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:22.180
but we also know that there's
much gender inequality

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:24.680
throughout the system with high levels

00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:27.610
of gender-based violence and disparities

00:31:27.610 --> 00:31:29.773
and education for women and girls.

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:32.760
Liberia's health care workforce also

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:34.970
has gender inequity, particularly

00:31:34.970 --> 00:31:37.823
in the higher paying jobs
and positions of leadership.

00:31:39.070 --> 00:31:41.740
This problem is certainly
not unique to Liberia,

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:45.780
we face the same challenges
here in the US and globally

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:48.870
but I wanted to ask about
strategies as you're thinking

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:53.140
about the educational pipeline,
health workforce development

00:31:53.140 --> 00:31:56.800
what are you using to
improve gender equity

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:58.683
in Liberia's health systems?

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:05.510
- Thank you, Kristina.

00:32:08.350 --> 00:32:13.350
It is true that women in
Liberia have held high positions

00:32:16.066 --> 00:32:19.783
in leadership, have occupied
positions in leadership.

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:25.470
And it is also true that gender inequity

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:27.623
is also high in Liberia.

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:34.343
The first thing is the culture,

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:41.480
we grew up knowing that we
live in a male-dominated, well

00:32:44.500 --> 00:32:49.500
male-dominated society,
women have defined roles.

00:32:51.190 --> 00:32:55.497
And so the women themselves also think

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:03.313
certain jobs are for men
and it is not for them.

00:33:05.750 --> 00:33:07.620
The previous administration

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:09.910
on the Madam Ellen Johnson Sirleaf

00:33:10.925 --> 00:33:13.843
did a lot of work promoting
female leadership.

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:21.130
That is not the same with
the government we have to do.

00:33:21.730 --> 00:33:25.420
There were more females
in leadership position

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:28.730
in the previous administration compared

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:31.533
to the current administration,

00:33:33.150 --> 00:33:35.600
the previous administration the females

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:38.620
in the previous administration
also had more voice

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:44.063
in leadership compared to
the administration today.

00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:50.963
So, how can we affect that for the future?

00:33:53.220 --> 00:33:56.709
At the moment, we are designing programs

00:33:56.709 --> 00:33:58.413
with some of our partners,

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:08.920
the program is to be able to
use policy to make decisions.

00:34:12.240 --> 00:34:16.363
And I mean data to make policy decisions.

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:20.750
Normally, if you look
at the different levels

00:34:20.750 --> 00:34:22.180
of governance in our country,

00:34:22.180 --> 00:34:24.633
if you take the law makers for example.

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:29.010
Often what you will hear from them is,

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:30.673
decision making is numbers.

00:34:31.970 --> 00:34:33.283
So for them the vote,

00:34:34.830 --> 00:34:38.120
and there are very few women in there.

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:40.743
So their voices are not heard,

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:44.520
and then the way I see law makers doing

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:47.940
their voting too, is I'm
supporting you today because

00:34:47.940 --> 00:34:51.463
when my time comes, you will
have to support me also.

00:34:52.579 --> 00:34:53.633
that's the politics.

00:34:55.430 --> 00:35:00.430
When it comes to the executive,
it's more of leadership,

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:05.710
how can it women provide
leadership to make policy decisions

00:35:05.710 --> 00:35:09.660
that will affect women move positively

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:11.963
and promote women's leadership.

00:35:12.980 --> 00:35:15.580
And so in our trainings moving forward

00:35:16.450 --> 00:35:21.450
is to design trainings for
professionals for policy makers

00:35:25.170 --> 00:35:27.320
and we'll specifically target

00:35:27.320 --> 00:35:29.573
more women to do that training.

00:35:30.510 --> 00:35:33.120
So we'll be able to build the skills

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:35.360
that they need to network, advocate

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:39.583
and maneuver their way in society.

00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:45.270
So, we purposefully designing the program

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:48.240
to target more women and people

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:51.440
who are at more are disadvantaged

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:55.223
as compared to just leaving it open.

00:35:56.650 --> 00:35:59.810
Sometimes you design the
program without the gender

00:35:59.810 --> 00:36:02.440
thinking about the agenda perspective.

00:36:02.440 --> 00:36:05.440
In the design of this program,

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:09.770
we look at data, we look at data from GIZ

00:36:09.770 --> 00:36:12.893
one of the partners on
gender in the health sector.

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:18.320
And we realized that
all the M and E officers

00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:20.463
in the 15 counties are males.

00:36:22.940 --> 00:36:24.810
There were like three different positions

00:36:24.810 --> 00:36:26.270
that they were on males.

00:36:26.270 --> 00:36:28.100
And then I just reflected and I said

00:36:28.100 --> 00:36:30.940
but this or that, or
no, my administration.

00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:34.620
So then I began to think,
how did this happen?

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:37.323
But the design of our project did not,

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:44.080
did not emphasize the gender
equity and how to address them.

00:36:45.500 --> 00:36:50.500
And so thinking loud,
maybe the M and E officers

00:36:51.410 --> 00:36:54.540
knowing that we work in an environment

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:56.570
where that is very challenging,

00:36:56.570 --> 00:37:00.720
you have to move, most of
the time they use motorbikes

00:37:00.720 --> 00:37:05.720
to reach to places, so
maybe they recruit males

00:37:05.780 --> 00:37:08.860
subconsciously take
that into consideration

00:37:08.860 --> 00:37:10.970
and top on people who will
be able to ride motorbike

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:12.900
to reach our facilities.

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:15.250
But you can train women
to ride motorbikes,

00:37:15.250 --> 00:37:17.864
so why we didn't do
that in the first place.

00:37:17.864 --> 00:37:18.760
(laughs)

00:37:18.760 --> 00:37:21.790
So, we were purposefully
designed our programs

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:25.560
to empower women to be able
to hold some of the positions

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:27.610
and to be able to do the kind of work

00:37:27.610 --> 00:37:30.053
that we normally think only men can do.

00:37:33.535 --> 00:37:36.040
- That was great, Dr. Dahn, and, I think,

00:37:37.139 --> 00:37:41.600
one of the things I've
been pleasantly observing

00:37:41.600 --> 00:37:45.300
in Liberia is just how
there's much freedom of speech

00:37:45.300 --> 00:37:48.100
and people feel very
comfortable airing their views

00:37:48.100 --> 00:37:50.600
even in the position
and to people of power.

00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:52.310
And I hope that, we can continue

00:37:52.310 --> 00:37:57.080
to advocacy for the rights and
status of women in Liberia.

00:37:57.080 --> 00:37:59.010
I have another question for you,

00:37:59.010 --> 00:38:02.770
before we try to tackle
some of the Q &amp; A questions.

00:38:02.770 --> 00:38:05.990
So I know that the, the
healthcare workforce program

00:38:05.990 --> 00:38:07.890
which you helped design and implement

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:10.220
relies heavily on external support

00:38:10.220 --> 00:38:12.860
both funding wise and
partners like you mentioned

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:15.920
but I think one thing
unique we've done in Liberia

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:18.240
which you somehow touched on, but we'd

00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:21.350
like you to expand on
is how we've had support

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:23.124
from within the West Africa region.

00:38:23.124 --> 00:38:26.770
So countries like Nigeria and Ghana,

00:38:26.770 --> 00:38:29.910
and how they provided really
valuable technical support

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:32.290
and resources to Liberia's health systems

00:38:32.290 --> 00:38:33.310
and medical education.

00:38:33.310 --> 00:38:35.257
So, what are your thoughts

00:38:35.257 --> 00:38:38.063
around advantages of this regional model,

00:38:38.063 --> 00:38:41.880
where countries within the
region supports each other,

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:43.520
and also given that they share

00:38:43.520 --> 00:38:47.323
similar limitations and lack of resources.

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:50.663
- Thank you.

00:38:53.315 --> 00:38:56.310
Like I said, in my previous statements

00:38:57.640 --> 00:38:59.260
the program was designed based

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:01.839
on the West African model because we live

00:39:01.839 --> 00:39:06.420
in the region and we have to also follow

00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:08.693
link to what is happening
within the region,

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:13.260
the design itself, we pay visits

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:16.143
so universities within the region also.

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:21.110
We expressed our vision to them

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:24.103
and they also decided they will help us.

00:39:25.520 --> 00:39:30.520
So the advantage of
having the West African

00:39:30.700 --> 00:39:35.700
college faculty members
to do the training for us

00:39:37.270 --> 00:39:41.793
is that the curriculum is similar.

00:39:43.084 --> 00:39:45.693
The work environment is similar,

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:51.830
and they can navigate
very difficult situations

00:39:51.830 --> 00:39:55.360
as compared to if you bring
somebody from the West

00:39:55.360 --> 00:39:58.610
who will have everything
at the level to them

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:04.870
and will not be able to wet your setting

00:40:04.870 --> 00:40:06.943
where most things are lacking.

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:10.090
As compared to someone who went

00:40:10.090 --> 00:40:13.593
to school in Ghana or Nigeria,

00:40:14.560 --> 00:40:18.683
who did not have so much sophistication,

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:22.733
the situation is especially similar.

00:40:24.050 --> 00:40:26.973
They know how to be innovative
and get things done,

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:30.370
and they will be able to use

00:40:30.370 --> 00:40:32.890
the limited resources that are available

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:35.947
to teach the way they learn it,

00:40:35.947 --> 00:40:38.410
and to be able to impact the knowledge

00:40:38.410 --> 00:40:41.099
that you want to have.

00:40:41.099 --> 00:40:44.203
And at the end of the day, it
ends up with the same results.

00:40:46.120 --> 00:40:47.980
So that is a very big advantage,

00:40:47.980 --> 00:40:50.250
and because we have the
West African college,

00:40:50.250 --> 00:40:53.240
you will realize that our performance also

00:40:53.240 --> 00:40:55.370
in the West African college exams,

00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:58.010
for membership and
fellowship is also very good.

00:40:59.430 --> 00:41:04.320
We've had situations
where our members went

00:41:04.320 --> 00:41:06.530
like surgery this year, all those who went

00:41:06.530 --> 00:41:08.140
from membership exam passed.

00:41:08.140 --> 00:41:11.390
All those who went for final exam passed,

00:41:11.390 --> 00:41:13.340
we've had situations where all those

00:41:13.340 --> 00:41:15.530
who went for pediatrics passed in the past

00:41:15.530 --> 00:41:18.190
and things like that because

00:41:18.190 --> 00:41:23.190
the faculty members know the terrain,

00:41:23.630 --> 00:41:28.594
they know the situation is and
so they adapt their training,

00:41:28.594 --> 00:41:32.313
to the real practical
realities on the ground.

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:38.190
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:41:38.190 --> 00:41:42.500
So we'll pivot and take
questions from the audience here.

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:46.610
Someone is interested in
how students are recruited

00:41:46.610 --> 00:41:50.023
for the science camp that
you were describing earlier.

00:41:54.290 --> 00:41:55.357
- Okay, thank you.

00:41:56.640 --> 00:41:59.783
The students are not yet recruited,

00:41:59.783 --> 00:42:02.243
that science camp was start 2021,

00:42:04.270 --> 00:42:07.433
but we will be recruiting
from the 15 counties.

00:42:09.480 --> 00:42:13.147
Initially we had engaged the AP cops

00:42:14.492 --> 00:42:16.744
because they were in
country, in we're teaching

00:42:16.744 --> 00:42:19.094
in the 15 we were teaching,
he did 15 counties.

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:25.010
And so we will work with
those who are reaching us

00:42:26.090 --> 00:42:28.943
to identify the best science students.

00:42:30.190 --> 00:42:34.970
We well purposefully ask
them to prioritize females

00:42:36.810 --> 00:42:40.430
and we will recruit them
into the camp for training.

00:42:40.430 --> 00:42:42.890
So we'll bring them to a central location

00:42:43.740 --> 00:42:48.740
for the vacation period and
build the kind of skills

00:42:49.120 --> 00:42:51.560
teaching them and building
the kind of skills

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:54.580
that are required for medical school,

00:42:54.580 --> 00:42:59.580
critical thinking, writing
skills and those kinds of things

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:03.920
in addition to the sciences

00:43:03.920 --> 00:43:05.703
that we are learning in the school.

00:43:10.400 --> 00:43:11.613
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn.

00:43:11.613 --> 00:43:14.680
There's actually a very
fascinating question.

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:17.600
I'm going to throw at
you from the audience.

00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:22.257
We've focused on building
health systems and,

00:43:22.257 --> 00:43:25.710
one aspect of that is that people

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:27.900
should have trust in the systems

00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:29.590
that they can receive adequate care

00:43:29.590 --> 00:43:32.480
and also trust in what health authorities,

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:33.760
which are very very critical,

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:35.610
particularly during a pandemic.

00:43:35.610 --> 00:43:38.680
So someone's asking a naughty question

00:43:38.680 --> 00:43:41.760
and that is what advice
would you offer the US,

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:45.210
regarding rebuilding
the trust of the public

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:48.030
after seemingly contradictory messaging

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:50.831
during the COVID-19 pandemic?

00:43:50.831 --> 00:43:54.110
So this is all about,
messaging and rebuilding trust.

00:43:54.110 --> 00:43:58.377
You know how in the US, the
loss of faith in systems,

00:43:58.377 --> 00:44:01.210
kinda impacts people's willingness

00:44:01.210 --> 00:44:03.000
to adhere to public health measures

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:04.510
take a vaccine if it's approved.

00:44:04.510 --> 00:44:06.520
So, given your experience as the Minister

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:09.120
of Health of Liberia, what
advice would you offer us

00:44:09.120 --> 00:44:12.923
about how ways to rebuild the
public trust in our systems?

00:44:14.450 --> 00:44:15.670
- Okay, thank you.

00:44:15.670 --> 00:44:19.710
Loss of public trust,
especially during the pandemic,

00:44:19.710 --> 00:44:21.946
the US is not the first.

00:44:21.946 --> 00:44:24.393
During the Ebola outbreak,

00:44:24.393 --> 00:44:27.280
(laughs)

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:29.823
a similar thing happened in Liberia.

00:44:30.660 --> 00:44:33.823
The public did not trust we had Ebola

00:44:35.435 --> 00:44:40.040
and so they did I have
trust in the government.

00:44:40.040 --> 00:44:42.770
People believe that the government

00:44:43.640 --> 00:44:47.173
was making up stories to
get more donor funding,

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:53.630
and so they did not adhere
to the health instructions

00:44:55.630 --> 00:44:57.880
that were coming from
the Ministry of Health,

00:44:59.050 --> 00:45:01.393
until Ebola overwhelmed us.

00:45:03.340 --> 00:45:07.420
The communities took upon
themselves after that

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:13.640
to organize themselves
to address the issues

00:45:14.070 --> 00:45:15.823
from the community level.

00:45:16.740 --> 00:45:18.503
So they were the ones doing the step

00:45:18.503 --> 00:45:20.900
by step health messages.

00:45:20.900 --> 00:45:23.430
They were the ones who
were identifying the cases

00:45:23.430 --> 00:45:26.640
for isolation, they were the ones

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:30.470
who were mobilizing resources
to support family members

00:45:30.470 --> 00:45:34.783
and communities who family
members got affected.

00:45:35.898 --> 00:45:39.223
That model is what we took,

00:45:41.740 --> 00:45:45.740
we revised our community health strategy

00:45:48.230 --> 00:45:51.853
and policy and that is what
we are implementing now.

00:45:53.130 --> 00:45:58.130
Now, what I've realized with
the outbreak of COVID-19,

00:46:01.350 --> 00:46:04.160
they like your scenario
and the US scenario,

00:46:04.160 --> 00:46:09.160
my own perspective is the not using data

00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:15.443
for decision making.

00:46:19.458 --> 00:46:24.030
The technicians are
there, the US government

00:46:24.030 --> 00:46:25.680
and that's the interesting thing

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:29.750
about it was the key partner who helped us

00:46:30.590 --> 00:46:34.140
to establish the incident
management system,

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:36.363
the emergency operation centers,

00:46:37.526 --> 00:46:42.526
the surveillance systems
that we have in place today.

00:46:42.690 --> 00:46:45.423
that is addressing the COVID issues.

00:46:47.830 --> 00:46:50.370
What I see with the US,
those systems are there

00:46:51.468 --> 00:46:53.940
they are routine healthcare
systems are there.

00:46:53.940 --> 00:46:56.733
The emergency systems are also there.

00:46:58.260 --> 00:47:01.250
The difference I see here is they have

00:47:01.250 --> 00:47:03.520
also decentralized our system.

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:08.443
And so today the county help
teams respond to emergencies,

00:47:09.310 --> 00:47:11.780
they start responding to emergencies

00:47:11.780 --> 00:47:16.123
before the national public
health institute goes to assist.

00:47:17.713 --> 00:47:19.200
And I think that is the difference,

00:47:19.200 --> 00:47:24.200
with the CDC sending
teams to states to help.

00:47:27.900 --> 00:47:30.613
Another thing I see is the politics,

00:47:32.130 --> 00:47:35.130
the politics, the government,

00:47:35.130 --> 00:47:36.860
and this happens with the government.

00:47:36.860 --> 00:47:40.410
It is so sad to say that politicians

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:45.410
will use the population to remain

00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:50.943
in power or to gain popularity.

00:47:52.630 --> 00:47:56.920
The technicians are giving
information and giving guidance

00:47:56.920 --> 00:48:00.750
but politicians want
to hold onto positions

00:48:00.750 --> 00:48:02.770
want to be seen as working,

00:48:02.770 --> 00:48:05.860
and so they'll leave audit data aside

00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:08.210
and they follow the politics as a result,

00:48:08.210 --> 00:48:11.840
things are not working as
it should work in the US,

00:48:11.840 --> 00:48:13.910
that's my own analysis that

00:48:13.910 --> 00:48:16.780
also happens here in Liberia sometimes.

00:48:16.780 --> 00:48:18.430
The politicians will take things,

00:48:18.430 --> 00:48:22.420
we have had hospitals that
were built in the past,

00:48:22.420 --> 00:48:25.663
because a politician wanted
a hospital in their village.

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:28.510
There's a hospital (mumbles)

00:48:28.510 --> 00:48:30.200
that one patient have never entered,

00:48:30.200 --> 00:48:32.860
a very big structure only because

00:48:32.860 --> 00:48:35.350
a politician wanted recognition.

00:48:35.350 --> 00:48:37.810
The politicians have to listen,

00:48:37.810 --> 00:48:41.203
to the technicians, they
have to work together

00:48:41.203 --> 00:48:43.980
in the interests of the population

00:48:43.980 --> 00:48:46.370
and not for themselves, thank you.

00:48:48.987 --> 00:48:52.609
- Thank you so much, Dr.
Dahn, it's very powerful.

00:48:52.609 --> 00:48:55.709
So we have just a few more minutes,

00:48:55.709 --> 00:49:00.140
wanted to ask you, we
have many individuals

00:49:00.140 --> 00:49:04.680
including students in the
US, in Liberia, all over,

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:06.690
who are passionate about the issues

00:49:06.690 --> 00:49:08.870
that we've been discussing today

00:49:08.870 --> 00:49:11.040
and who want to engage in this kind

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:14.160
of work after they graduate.

00:49:14.160 --> 00:49:17.850
And I wanted to ask what
would be your advice for them,

00:49:17.850 --> 00:49:20.430
competencies and skills
you think they need,

00:49:20.430 --> 00:49:22.770
or how you would advise them

00:49:22.770 --> 00:49:24.613
if they go forward in their careers.

00:49:27.630 --> 00:49:28.463
- Thank you.

00:49:30.580 --> 00:49:35.470
My own scenario, I was a
mid-level professional,

00:49:35.470 --> 00:49:38.340
so I graduated from medical school.

00:49:38.340 --> 00:49:41.340
I worked in the hospital for some time,

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:43.870
and at a certain time, I
went over to the ministry

00:49:43.870 --> 00:49:47.330
as a director without leadership skills

00:49:47.330 --> 00:49:51.789
or management skills and I
still did the clinical work

00:49:51.789 --> 00:49:54.577
alongside with the Ministry
of Public Health work

00:50:01.153 --> 00:50:03.223
until I went for my training,

00:50:05.270 --> 00:50:07.933
for my MBAs at a University of Washington,

00:50:09.210 --> 00:50:11.970
that training had a component

00:50:11.970 --> 00:50:15.143
that focused on leadership and management.

00:50:16.660 --> 00:50:19.530
In addition to, even though
leadership and management

00:50:19.530 --> 00:50:22.510
was built in all the topics

00:50:22.510 --> 00:50:24.860
even in the church, I had that.

00:50:24.860 --> 00:50:26.860
But there were specific sessions

00:50:26.860 --> 00:50:29.230
on leadership and management.

00:50:29.230 --> 00:50:34.230
And because I had practical experience

00:50:34.330 --> 00:50:39.330
prior to going to school,
it helped me to map out

00:50:39.596 --> 00:50:43.110
what my shortcomings were.

00:50:43.110 --> 00:50:47.123
So it reshaped the way our
work and the way I think,

00:50:48.330 --> 00:50:52.255
it helped me to develop advocacy skills.

00:50:52.255 --> 00:50:55.850
It helped me to learn how to network

00:50:57.670 --> 00:51:02.670
among partners, my supervisors,
leaders to get things done.

00:51:03.950 --> 00:51:07.450
So even if, say for
instance, if I have a boss

00:51:07.450 --> 00:51:10.560
and I have an addition that my boss

00:51:10.560 --> 00:51:14.260
is finding difficult to I can actually get

00:51:14.260 --> 00:51:17.250
to another person who I
think my boss would listen to

00:51:18.280 --> 00:51:21.840
and that person can help to
position my vision for me

00:51:23.450 --> 00:51:25.240
Communication skill is key,

00:51:25.240 --> 00:51:28.523
you must be able to
articulate your vision.

00:51:29.730 --> 00:51:31.990
You must be able to think strategically

00:51:31.990 --> 00:51:35.199
and must be able to think ahead.

00:51:35.199 --> 00:51:38.770
That's where you are
able to position things.

00:51:38.770 --> 00:51:41.650
You are able to navigate the system

00:51:41.650 --> 00:51:43.243
to be able to get things done.

00:51:44.260 --> 00:51:48.863
You must be able to know
who are your stakeholders,

00:51:49.970 --> 00:51:54.070
you should know who can
help you to be successful

00:51:54.070 --> 00:51:56.680
and who can help to break you

00:51:56.680 --> 00:51:59.589
and how you can bring them together.

00:51:59.589 --> 00:52:02.250
You must be innovative,

00:52:02.250 --> 00:52:06.100
you must be thinking
ahead, ahead of the work.

00:52:06.100 --> 00:52:08.430
So those are some of the
things that actually helped me

00:52:08.430 --> 00:52:13.430
and I actually learned that
after some years of practice

00:52:13.690 --> 00:52:17.443
went back to school I was
able to clearly define

00:52:19.220 --> 00:52:20.860
what I was doing previously

00:52:20.860 --> 00:52:24.360
and what I think I could do
moving forward, thank you.

00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:30.870
- Thank you, Dr. Dahn, I think
it's always just inspiring

00:52:30.870 --> 00:52:32.940
to see how you've
climbed through the ranks

00:52:32.940 --> 00:52:36.810
and really left a footprints
at all different levels

00:52:36.810 --> 00:52:38.680
of the health system.

00:52:38.680 --> 00:52:40.550
So I just have one quick question,

00:52:40.550 --> 00:52:43.010
we have just six minutes before we close.

00:52:43.010 --> 00:52:45.820
So before I hand off to Kristina

00:52:45.820 --> 00:52:47.600
to give a closing statement

00:52:47.600 --> 00:52:50.520
I couldn't resist asking
you one last question.

00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:53.213
So, have current challenges
including COVID-19

00:52:53.213 --> 00:52:55.690
and you've got, showed leadership

00:52:55.690 --> 00:52:58.550
during the time of Ebola for your country.

00:52:58.550 --> 00:53:00.690
And so I would just like you to

00:53:00.690 --> 00:53:03.090
in the next two, three minutes, just,

00:53:03.090 --> 00:53:05.260
share with us your thoughts as to

00:53:05.260 --> 00:53:09.090
what you think are the next big challenges

00:53:09.090 --> 00:53:11.530
for global health in the
next five to 10 years.

00:53:11.530 --> 00:53:14.163
So, both within the Liberian context

00:53:14.163 --> 00:53:16.890
and maybe US context, we see, for example

00:53:16.890 --> 00:53:19.540
for an infectious disease,
like COVID-19 how,

00:53:19.540 --> 00:53:21.870
our destinies are tied globally.

00:53:21.870 --> 00:53:24.400
And, that we really have to work together

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:26.680
to confront of our biggest challenges.

00:53:26.680 --> 00:53:29.420
So, what do you see in
the next five to 10 years

00:53:29.420 --> 00:53:33.573
as being, future global health challenges?

00:53:35.090 --> 00:53:39.983
- Well, pandemics will always be with us,

00:53:42.180 --> 00:53:45.080
just how we are trying
to solve the problems

00:53:45.080 --> 00:53:48.550
with disease outbreak is the same way

00:53:48.550 --> 00:53:50.736
that pathogens are strategizing

00:53:50.736 --> 00:53:54.673
to challenge us with new outbreaks.

00:53:55.760 --> 00:53:58.420
And so, as we design systems

00:53:58.420 --> 00:54:01.603
we should remember that we
need to design the systems.

00:54:02.730 --> 00:54:03.930
Yes, emergency respond to it

00:54:03.930 --> 00:54:07.780
but think long term,
because you will always

00:54:07.780 --> 00:54:11.660
have an outbreak that
you have to respond to.

00:54:11.660 --> 00:54:12.840
At least in my lifetime,

00:54:12.840 --> 00:54:17.840
I've seen HIV, Bad Flu,
(indistinct) disease,

00:54:20.131 --> 00:54:21.760
COVID 19, Ebola, I've heard some,

00:54:21.760 --> 00:54:25.040
I've seen some, so it's like

00:54:25.040 --> 00:54:26.920
we'll continue to have our outbreaks.

00:54:26.920 --> 00:54:31.920
And so building the
systems is key to managing

00:54:33.614 --> 00:54:37.783
both the routine services
and the outbreaks.

00:54:38.690 --> 00:54:42.010
So there's a need for us to not to give up

00:54:42.010 --> 00:54:45.694
on building a system once
that things come back,

00:54:45.694 --> 00:54:47.730
return to normal, we forget

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:50.420
and then we go back to
our regular routine.

00:54:50.420 --> 00:54:52.450
We should always have the systems

00:54:52.450 --> 00:54:55.740
to be able to provide
routine healthcare services

00:54:55.740 --> 00:54:57.895
and be able to respond to emergency

00:54:57.895 --> 00:55:01.025
when there's an outbreak.

00:55:01.025 --> 00:55:06.025
Another challenge is the it's
non-communicable diseases

00:55:09.320 --> 00:55:12.778
that is also growing gradually,

00:55:12.778 --> 00:55:14.980
somehow in the developed world.

00:55:14.980 --> 00:55:16.663
It has been there for some time,

00:55:17.560 --> 00:55:21.470
but in the developing
world, it is growing.

00:55:21.470 --> 00:55:25.470
And for us in the developing world

00:55:25.470 --> 00:55:27.400
to manage these things is expensive,

00:55:27.400 --> 00:55:31.300
so we need to think on
preventives seriously.

00:55:31.300 --> 00:55:33.040
So those are the two key things

00:55:33.040 --> 00:55:36.700
that I think we should look
into moving into the future.

00:55:36.700 --> 00:55:37.533
Thank you.

